LEAD = {
    "headline": "FOR HALF OF PODCAST FANS, A ONE-OFF BRAND MENTION READS AS A SIGN THE HOST DOES NOT ACTUALLY USE THE PRODUCT.  FIFTY-NINE PERCENT AMONG GEN Z.  AND A SECOND STUDY FOUR DAYS LATER FOUND THE HOST READ IS QUIETLY PRICING THE ORDINARY AD THAT FOLLOWS IT",
    "deck": "Two separate surveys landed inside four days and both point at the same thing.  The single creator integration, which is how almost every brand buys one, is not a small win.  For half the audience it is read as proof the host does not use your product, and the thing that reverses that is repetition rather than better creative.",
    "stamps": [
        ("NET INFLUENCER · 16 OCT", "https://www.netinfluencer.com/podcast-fans-trust-creator-hosts-more-than-their-own-friends-survey-finds/"),
        ("NET INFLUENCER · 19 OCT", "https://www.netinfluencer.com/more-than-half-of-podcast-listeners-say-ad-load-is-too-high-favor-host-reads-free-membership-tiers-study-finds/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "One piece of vocabulary before anything else, because the whole item turns on it.  A host read is when the person presenting the show says your advertising copy themselves, in their own voice, inside the programme — as against a pre-recorded spot, which is an ordinary advert dropped into a break.  On YouTube this matters more than it sounds, because video podcasts are brand-owned and creator-owned video, and the host read is the single most commonly bought unit in the whole creator market.",
        "The first study was fielded by SmithGeiger Group for Amazon's Wondery in June and written up on Friday.  Three thousand United States adults aged 18 to 64 who regularly watch or listen to hosted podcasts or online series, balanced between men and women, 30 percent Gen Z, 40 percent Millennial, 30 percent Gen X.  Each respondent answered about one show and one host, their own stated favourite.  The trust numbers are enormous and broadly what you would expect: <mark>81 percent trust the host as much as or more than their own friends, and 44 percent trust the host more</mark>.  Eighty-five percent say the host feels like a friend.  Seventy-six percent say they do not mind hearing adverts in the show if it supports the host.",
        "Then the finding that should change what you buy.  <mark>For half of the fans surveyed, a one-off brand mention signals that the host may not actually use or like the product</mark> — rising to 59 percent among Gen Z and 54 percent among Millennials.  Not neutral.  Not a modest effect.  Evidence against you, drawn from the fact that you turned up once.  And the fix in the data is not craft, it is frequency: <mark>71 percent say a brand appearing across multiple episodes feels like a genuine partnership rather than an ad read</mark>, 78 percent say that sustained presence changes how they think about the brand, 70 percent say it makes them more likely to trust it and 67 percent more likely to try it.  Two in three say they associate a brand with the host or the show when they meet it somewhere else in the day.",
        "The second study arrived this morning and comes at the same unit from a different direction.  The Podcast Study 2026, from Strategic Solutions Research and Point-To-Point Marketing, 1,205 podcast listeners.  Host reads strongly influence a decision to try or buy for 40 percent of listeners against 27 percent for pre-recorded spots, and 55 percent say they listen all the way through a host read.  Those two you could have guessed.  This one you could not: <mark>51 percent say a host read makes them more likely to notice a standard ad later in the same show, and 43 percent say it increases their trust in the ad that follows it</mark>.",
        "Read that as a pricing statement, because that is what it is.  If the host read lifts attention to and trust in whatever runs after it, then the position of the host read in the running order is setting the value of the inventory next to it, and nobody is paying for that.  The same study has 55 percent of listeners already saying there are too many adverts in their feeds, only 8.6 percent willing to tolerate more, and 39 percent saying they consume less, pay or would pay to avoid adverts, or have stopped — against just 7 percent who actually abandoned a show over it.  The report's own word for that pattern is erosion rather than abandonment, which is the honest description.",
        "Put the two together and the mechanism is clear enough to act on.  The host's credibility is not a thing you rent for one episode; it is a thing the audience lends you once it believes the host would use the product without being paid, and a single appearance is the strongest available evidence that they would not.  Three months of presence in one show buys you something a single appearance across twelve shows cannot, for the same money.  That is the opposite of how creator plans are built, which is breadth first, because breadth is what reach-based planning rewards and because one-off integrations are easier to buy, easier to approve and easier to report.",
        "One more number worth keeping, from the Wondery work, because it is about how discovery actually happens.  Short clips are the top driver of finding a new show, described as nearly twice as effective as word of mouth, and among fans who watch clips <mark>95 percent take some action afterwards</mark> — 41 percent talk about it, 37 percent share it directly, and only 24 percent recall seeing a brand or logo inside the clip itself.  So the clip is where the audience arrives and the place your branding is least likely to survive the trip.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("50%", "of podcast fans read a one-off brand mention as a sign the host does not use the product"),
        ("71%", "say a brand across multiple episodes feels like a partnership rather than an ad read"),
        ("51%", "say a host read makes them more likely to notice the ordinary ad that follows it"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Both studies are paid for by companies that sell what the findings recommend.  Wondery is Amazon's podcast arm and sells exactly the multi-episode inventory its study concludes you should buy; its survey was fielded in June and surfaced in October, and the Nielsen brand-lift meta-analysis it cites alongside covers campaigns Wondery itself commissioned.  Point-To-Point Marketing sells podcast marketing and audience-growth services, and the study's action plan — cap the ad load, price host reads above standard spots, build a membership tier and a host email list — maps onto its own business.  Everything in both is self-reported stated preference rather than measured behaviour, neither has been published as a standalone paper anybody can audit, and the 1,205-listener study publishes no margin of error.  The 50-against-71 finding is used here because it cuts against the easy sale rather than for it.",
    "so_what": "The one-off creator integration is the default purchase in this market and both of these studies say it is the worst available use of the money, for a reason that has nothing to do with the creative.  Trust in a host transfers to a brand on repetition, not on contact, and a single appearance actively supplies the audience with a reason to discount you.  The second study adds the part nobody is pricing: the host read is not just a better-performing unit, it is a primer that raises the value of whatever runs after it, which means the running order of a show is a commercial decision being made by an editor.",
    "do_this": "Take your next creator budget and spend all of it on one third as many shows for three times as long, so every show you are in sees you across at least three consecutive episodes.  Write a minimum episode count into your creator template this week and make a single-episode buy the exception that needs an argument.  And when you negotiate a host read, ask what runs immediately after it and price that slot too, because on this data you are already paying to improve it.",
}

